 color but we're basically  scanning top to bottom in download not  surprised only the camera app puts top  to bottom  similarly in up mode we're going to tell  the sensor to read from the bottom of  the sensor and go up so the base of the  triangle arrives first the point arrives  last and what the camera outputs is as  you would expect  no one else has implemented down up mode  and the benefit here is there's no  flyback time in this galvo we've got  here because we're going to scan down  and then up and then down and up so what  it's going to actually arrive at the  sensor from your image are a series of  inverted images but the camera knows  what it's doing here so what the actual  camera is going to Output is going to  flip every other image so that you don't  have to do that after the fact thank you  for your time  have a good day  thanks again to Phil for taking us  through the kinetics Prime BSI and Iris  families of CMOS cameras for light sheet  microscopy to follow up on this we have  some real life examples of the kinetics  and Iris cameras being used for light  sheet microscopy  our first customer story is also from  Professor kabitzkek this application  concerns super resolution expansion  light sheet where essentially samples  are physically magnified via expansion  and then you can perform light sheet on  them and Achieve super resolution  results  the main takeaway here is that once you  have these much larger samples thanks to  the expansion process you now need to  make sure your Imaging field is  sufficiently large so you're not  constantly stitching and tiling  different images together and adding  lots of time onto your experimentation  in image a we can see it takes a typical  CMOS camera 10 Acquisitions to capture  this field  each one of these Acquisitions is in  multiple colors you have overlap between  each image the X Y needs to scan across  the more images you're taking the more  time it's taking the more you're  exposing your sample and the longer your  experiments are taking it's just not  very efficient with the kinetics you  need to take two Acquisitions this  vastly cuts down on the amount of time  taken and allows you to be a lot more  efficient with the speed of the kinetics  also allowing you to run a higher  throughput because you can run this  experiment faster it's a very sensitive  camera so you can often use less light  dose and you can see from the pixel  array that you can still image at a very  high resolution as we see in the  magnified images C and D  so with the kinetics it really helps you  image these very large samples much  faster and much more efficiently  another example of the kinetics being  used for light sheet comes from Dr  Leonardo Ciccone Dr cecconi is Imaging  cleared Mouse Huts so the image here you  can see is a Clarity cleared Mouse Hut  often it would take again a good number  of different Acquisitions in order to  capture a very large sample  but by pairing the kinetics with a  custom 2x telecentric lens you can  capture a field of view like this in the  centimeter range allowing you to get a  whole picture of the heart in just one  acquisition in the whites Square here  you can see an image this is the entire  Mouse heart captured in just one  acquisition  the field of Illumination is very flat  the frame rates are very high and again  this really allows you to up the  efficiency and the throughput of your  experimentation because you can now  capture the heart in just one image  there's no need to scan there's no need  for tiling and again from the magnifying  image you can see this all happens at a  very high resolution so this simplifies  your experiments and it also makes them  more powerful with the kinetics  next we have two custom stories for the  prime BSI Express  this camera is a flexible compact  package with a industry standard 6.5  Micron pixel and a high speed making it  great for light sheet Imaging  our first story comes from Dr James  Madden from the University of Cambridge  who is using multiple prime BSI  expresses for subcellular oblique plane  microscopy this being one of the single  objective formats of light sheet using  the Mr snouty objective that allows you  to look at single cells and highly  sensitive samples at very high speeds  the prime BSI Express performs really  well in this application because it can  image at a very high resolution at a  high speed while maintaining a high  sensitivity thanks to the near perfect  Quantum efficiency and the low noise  modes  this customer story involves the lab of  Professor retifuka  this lab has developed a technique known  as axially swept light sheet microscopy  this technique excels in high resolution  3D imaging of subcellular structures  over large volumes  this is applied in this case to  endothelial cells that form blood  vessels inside zebrafish  with the prime BSI Express and  programmable scan mode  which Phil discussed  you can image using the reader of the  camera synchronized to the scan of the  light sheet and it allows you to really  maximize your Imaging time while  maintaining a really large field of view  at a high resolution again other high  sensitivity  overall the prime BSI Express is a  compact flexible and reliable package  for light sheet microscopy  we also have two examples of the iris 15  due to the very large field of view and  the small pixel the iris is a great fit  for light sheet microscopy this is Dr  Fabian Voigt with miso spin so the Miso  sperm is for miso scale microscopy this  is a very large samples and it's an open  science project aimed at making these  light sheet microscopes for very large  samples as you can probably tell this is  a full Mouse brain again this is cleared  the mouse brain has been captured within  one acquisition on the iris so the large  detector size and the rectangular sensor  of the iris really allow you to capture  a lot in a field of view at a very high  resolution which allows you to capture  fine features especially in a sample as  complex as a brain  and lastly we have Dr Yan hoiskin it  really speaks for itself this is a  cleared zebrafish and you're capturing a  huge amount of it again in one single  acquisition with the rs-15 so the large  chip and the small pixel you can really  optimize this for low magnification  Imaging and now you can image huge  samples like this at very high  resolution again without zooming and  without having to add additional  components onto experimentation  and that just about covers everything we  wanted to talk about today  just to summarize things light sheet is  a highly versatile technique with many  different variations scmos cameras we  present different product lines which  have large sensors and very high speeds  which allow you to make your experiments  higher throughput and more efficient  while also simplifying them different  software components like programmable  scan mode allow you to have advanced  readout control you have the hardware to  do the Imaging but you also have the  software to do more powerful Imaging and  have greater control over it especially  pairing it with the illumination by  pairing the readout of the camera with  the illumination you can further cut  down on the light dose and really run  longer physiological experiments over  the long term without damaging your  samples  and again there's the kinetics  with the kinetics there's just no need  to compromise you get it all you get the  sensitivity the speed the field of view  and the high resolution it's great for  light sheet we also offer the prime BSI  and Iris lines which also perform  extremely well for light sheet and match  the different applications and different  samples that you may want to image  but that's all from us today for this  webinar if you have any questions please  let us know and thank you so much for  attending  thank you Professor kubicek Dr Kumar Dr  koston and Dr Allen for your informative  presentatio